  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV10">
          <l>tiṉaiyē taruppaṇa mutirai cennel</l>
          <l>vilaikō ḷiṉaiyaṉa<anchor xml:id="inaiyana"/> mēviya vuṇavē.<anchor xml:id="una"/></l>
</lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l>Millet,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṉai</hi></q> can also refer to millets in general, not just the foxtail type mentioned in the commentary to the previous 
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>. As this is an aphorism, not a detailed commentary 
  enumeration, I have chosen the more general term, though as this is the millet associated with the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kuṟiñci</hi></term> setting, it may well be referring to the specific foxtail millet. This translation is more in keeping with the other items such 
  as ‘legumes’.</note> refreshments,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">From Skt. <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tarpaṇa</hi></term>, which
    originally meant ‘a libation to the gods’. I think this meaning has been extended into
    ‘refreshments’, in the sense of cooling foods and drinks, though that is purely
    speculation.</note> legumes, red rice, and exchanged goods</l>
    <l> – such things are the foods attached [to the poetic settings].</l></lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV10comm1">eṉpatuṇavām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the food occurs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV10comm2">tiṉaiyun taruppaṇamum varaku mutaliyaṉavum vayal viḷaiyuñ cen nellum 
      uppu vilaiyum<anchor xml:id="uppuvilai"/> uḷ ḷiṭṭaṉa vellām uṇavām eṉṟa vāṟu.</p>
      <p xml:lang="en">Foxtail millet, refreshments, those things beginning with common millet, red rice 
        grown in paddy fields, and salt for trade – all [of these] are included as the foods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
        <p xml:id="TNV10comm3">iṉaiyaṉa veṉṟataṉāl vetir nelluṅ kiḻaṅkun tēṉeyyum aruvi nīruñ cuṉai nīrum<anchor xml:id="kurinci"/> 
      āṟalaittaṉavum vaṟuñ cuṉai nīrum uvari nīruṅ<anchor xml:id="palai"/> kāṉ yāṟṟu nīrum<anchor xml:id="mullai"/> veṇ ṇelluṅ kiṇaṟṟu nīrum poykai<anchor xml:id="marutam"/> nīrumaṇaṟ 
      kūvaṉīrum<anchor xml:id="neytal"/> uḷ ḷiṭṭaṉa vellāṅ koḷka.<anchor xml:id="note"/></p>
      <p xml:lang="en">By <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iṉaiyaṉa</hi></q>, understand that all [of these] are included: bamboo-rice,<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">Bamboo-rice (<q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vetir nel</hi></q>, also <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">veyir nel</hi></q>) refers to the grain shed by flowering bamboo, traditionally gathered as a food in mountain regions. On the gloss of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vetir nel</hi></q> as bamboo-paddy, see Aṉantarāmaiyar (1925, 250).</note> root vegetables, honey, waterfall water, mountain-spring water; things stolen on the road, 
      water from dried mountain springs, brackish water; water from a forest river; white rice, 
      water from wells, pond water; water from wells in the sand.</p>
      </div>
      </body>
      </text>
